 implies the printed one. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy"],"id":"long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 6.31 (The half-skip dichotomy via Erdős–Borwein irrationality)","locator":"Long record Theorem 6.31, p. 53, label thm:half-skip-dichotomy","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*The half target satisfies\n$$\n\\frac12\\in\\mathcal A\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\mathbb N_{>0}\\smallsetminus G\\text{ is infinite},\n$$\nwhere $G$ is the greedy support for $1/2$. In the forward direction, finitely many skipped exponents would express the full Mersenne sum as $1/2$ plus a finite rational sum, contradicting Erdős’s full-support irrationality theorem. The reverse implication uses the greedy tail criterion: once a remainder exceeds the whole available tail, every later exponent is selected. Infinitely many skips exclude that failure. The equivalence does not establish that infinitely many skips actually occur.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy","id":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.half_mem_mersenneAchievementSet_iff_greedySkippedSupport_infinite%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-half-skip-dichotomy%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.half_mem_mersenneAchievementSet_iff_greedySkippedSupport_infinite%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","semantic_class":"formalization_correspondence","to":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc32